course: dictionary
lesson: 13536
title: Reading between the lines
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To imply something means to suggest it without saying it directly. The speaker hints, and the listener works out the rest. «Mum looked at the empty biscuit tin and said, "Someone has been busy today."» Mum does not say "you ate them all", but her words imply it.
explain: The listener has to read between the lines. Ask yourself: what is the speaker really getting at? «Tom's sister said, "Oh good, you finally tidied your room."» She implies that Tom's room was messy before, and she is pleased, though she is also teasing him.
explain: In logic and maths, imply has a different job. It means that if one thing is true, another thing must also be true. «Being a square implies having four sides.» If a shape is a square, you know for certain it has four sides. This is an "if... then" link.
explain: Useful phrases to spot are «imply that», «what are you implying?» and «is implied by». When someone asks, "What are you implying?", they want to know the hidden meaning behind your words.
fact: To imply means to suggest something without saying it straight out.
fact: «Are you implying I'm lazy?» means "Are you hinting that I'm lazy, without saying it?"
fact: The person who hints is the one who implies; the person who works out the hidden meaning is the one who infers.
fact: In maths and logic, «A implies B» means that if A is true, then B must also be true.
fact: Imply is a verb: imply, implies, implied, implying.
exercise: «Mum said, "Someone has been busy with the biscuits."» What does Mum imply? | *Someone has eaten a lot of biscuits | Someone has gone to the shop | Someone has lost the tin | Someone has baked a new cake
exercise: «Being a square implies having four sides.» In this sentence, what does implies mean? | It is a square and also a circle | Four sides are better than three | Squares are always red | *If it is a square, it must have four sides
exercise: Which word means to suggest something without saying it directly? | obey | *imply | repeat | describe
test: «Are you implying that I told a lie?» What is the speaker asking? | *Is the other person hinting that they lied? | Is the other person saying they are very tall? | Is the other person asking for a drink? | Is the other person telling a joke about a lie?
test: «Being a rectangle implies having four corners.» If a shape is a rectangle, what must be true? | It is always blue | It has three sides | It has no corners at all | *It has four corners
exam: «His smile implied that he already knew the answer.» What does his smile suggest? | He was about to sneeze | He wanted a different answer | He had forgotten the question | *He knew the answer without saying so
